4. CRITERION-4 RENDERED-STRING SUMS, ONE ROUNDING PATH — PASS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
TestDashboardVerdictBar_RenderedFiguresReflectPlanExclusion
(plan_exclusions_render_test.go) is a real end-to-end pipeline, not a
hand-built struct: a models.TransactionSet with FRACTIONAL-CENT amounts
(-1804.415 rent, -300.415 premium, -600.415 flagged) and a flagged def, run
through the real metrics.Calculate -> BuildBudgetVerdict ->
renderer.RenderToString("dashboard-verdict-bar"), then it extracts the
DOLLAR FIGURES BACK OUT OF THE RENDERED HTML:
    gotSpent := extractDollarAfter(t, out, "Spent</div>")
    total := extractDollarBefore(t, out, "over budget")
    living := extractDollarAfter(t, out, "Living:")
    healthcare := extractDollarAfter(t, out, "Healthcare:")
    if got, want := living+healthcare, total; math.Abs(got-want) > 0.001 {...}
So both halves of the rule are covered: the Spent figure reflects the
exclusion (`wantSpent := m.TotalExpenses - m.PlanExcludedTotal`), and the
rendered Living + Healthcare still sum to the rendered combined total to the
cent, now with SY4's arithmetic feeding the pipeline.

ONE ROUNDING PATH — I checked for the W2 dual-formatter class rather than
assuming its absence:
  $ grep -n "toLocaleString|toFixed|Math.round|formatMoney|formatNumber|Intl\." \
        budget-vs-actual.html kpis.html dashboard-verdict-bar.html
    -> every hit is a Go template {{formatMoney ...}}; ZERO JS number
       formatting in any of the three.
  $ grep -rn "toLocaleString|toFixed" web/static/js/
    -> charts.js:234 only, and I read its context: it formats a PERCENT on
       the donut legend row (`pctNum.toFixed(1) + '%'`).
  charts.js does contain two Intl currency formatters (lines ~207 and ~460),
  and I traced both rather than stopping at the grep: the first (fmtMoney)
  renders `it.amount` in the major-expense donut legend — a group spend
  total, which SY4 deliberately does not change; the second
  (formatCurrency) renders the what-if projection's final-balance label.
  NEITHER renders a living-budget figure. No living dollar figure is
  produced by two formatters, so there is no figure whose Go and JS
  renderings could disagree.

