FINDINGS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
F1 (durability gap, criterion 2/5 — the split-classification class itself).
   Three of the four production wiring points can be silently reverted to nil
   with the ENTIRE test suite staying green:
     handlers.go:129 handleDashboard, :218 handleKPIsPartial, :283
     handleChartData — each mutated to `planExclusions := nil`, and
     `go test ./internal/services/metrics/... ./internal/handlers/dashboard/...
      ./internal/services/mcpsvc/spend/...` returns ok/ok/ok every time.
   The reason is structural: the dashboard tests exercise the CONSUMERS
   directly — plan_exclusions_render_test.go calls metrics.Calculate itself
   and plan_exclusions_chart_test.go calls buildBudgetVsActualChartData
   itself, both passing an explicit map — so nothing ever executes the
   handler code that builds the map from data.Active(). The SY4 changes to
   handlers_test.go are purely mechanical `, nil` signature updates (I read
   the whole diff), adding no wiring coverage. mcpsvc/spend is the only site
   with a handler-level test, and it is the only one that fails.
   Today the wiring is CORRECT — I read all four sites and they build from
   the unfiltered set. This is not a defect in shipped behavior; it is an
   absence of a guard on the exact seam ruling 2026-08-29a says to guard.
   Recommended follow-up (the V3 "promote the probe" pattern): a test task
   seeding a flagged def into the loader's major_expenses.json, driving
   GET /dashboard and GET /dashboard/charts/data/budget-vs-actual through the
   router, and asserting the rendered/JSON living figure reflects the
   exclusion. My three overlay mutations are the ready-made calibration for
   it — each must fail the new test.
F2 (master health, PRE-EXISTING, outside SY4's scope — for the lead, not a
   FAIL). master at 4c3b65e does not compile its dataloader TEST package:
     $ git show master:internal/services/metrics/metrics.go | grep "^func Calculate"
       func Calculate(..., coverageStart time.Time, hasCoverage bool) *models.DashboardMetrics   [7 params]
     $ git show master:internal/services/dataloader/transfers_test.go | grep metrics.Calculate
       :137  metrics.Calculate(..., start, end, 0, 0)     [5 args]
       :280  metrics.Calculate(..., start, end, 0, 0)     [5 args]
   The HC merge changed the signature and left this caller behind — it was
   fenced as foreign territory at the time. `go build ./...` hides it (build
   ignores _test.go), which is why it survived the merge gate. The tree is
   green ONLY because SY4's uncommitted edit repairs it. Two consequences the
   lead should weigh: (i) SY4's transfers_test.go edit is load-bearing for
   master's health, not merely cosmetic, so it must not be dropped from the
   commit; (ii) a `go vet ./...` or `go test ./...` gate on master would have
   caught this at HC's merge, whereas `go build ./...` never will.
F3 (commit hygiene, not SY4's): an untracked 16MB ELF binary sits in the repo
   root — `budget2.old-1345` (Aug 30 13:45), and `git check-ignore` reports it
   is NOT ignored. A blind `git add -A` at commit time would commit it. I did
   not execute it (repo-root binaries port-kill). Not created by SY4; flagging
   for the commit step.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------
SUMMARY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
All five verifiable SY4 criteria are met, each on a quoted command's output.
The living-only design holds structurally (totals computed above the
subtraction) and is guarded by five independent mutations; nil == empty ==
no-match is a true golden reflect.DeepEqual over the whole struct; my own
grep enumeration of living-actual surfaces found nothing the manifest's 18
rows missed, all four map-building sites derive from the UNFILTERED set, and
the HI-first rule has exactly one implementation consumed by both surfaces;
the rendered-string test runs a fractional-cent flagged fixture through the
real pipeline and asserts on figures extracted from the HTML, with no living
figure formatted by JS anywhere. The one soft spot is F1: three of four
wiring points are unguarded by tests, which clears the criterion as written
(the fourth fails) but deserves a follow-up test task.
